<html xmlns:o='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office' xmlns:w='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40'><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>Narrative CV — working draft</title><style>@page Section1{size:8.5in 11in;margin:2cm;}div.Section1{page:Section1;}body{font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:12pt;line-height:1.2;color:#000;}.word-note{color:#555;font-style:italic;}h1{font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;margin:0 0 6pt;}h2{font-size:12pt;font-weight:bold;margin:12pt 0 4pt;}p{margin:0 0 6pt;}sup{font-weight:bold;} .contrib{margin:0 0 8pt;} .ctitle{font-weight:bold;}.evbox{border-left:2px solid #999;padding:0 0 0 8pt;margin:4pt 0 0;font-size:12pt;}.screen-note{font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;font-size:13px;color:#555;background:#fff8e1;border:1px solid #f9ca24;padding:8px 12px;margin:0 0 1.5rem;line-height:1.5;}@media print{.screen-note{display:none;} body{margin:0;padding:0;max-width:none;}}</style></head><body><div class='Section1'><p class='word-note'>Working draft from the Pathways narrative CV tool (V6 beta, 2026-10-23 (forked from V5)), set in 12-point Arial and headed with the section names of SSHRC's template. Paste each section into the matching section of the current version of SSHRC's template, keep within the page limit (5 pages in English, 6 pages in French), then delete this paragraph.</p><h2>Personal statement</h2><p>I am an associate professor at a Canadian university. My work is in urban water systems.</p><p>My work keeps returning to how cities prepare for floods with too little local data.</p><p>Modelling, partnerships and training run through one thread: usable local forecasts.</p><p>I came from civil engineering and moved toward policy after a flood in my home town.</p><p>Two Insight grants, 30 papers, and adoption by two cities.</p><p>This grant lets me test the model in ten small cities.</p><p>Next I will extend the work to small cities.</p><h2>Most significant contributions and experiences</h2><div class='contrib'><p><span class='ctitle'>1. Stormwater models for cities.</span> <em>I led the modelling and the municipal partnerships.</em> Thousands of basements flood each spring because cities lack local overflow forecasts. Since 2019 I built and tested an overflow model with three municipal partners. The project produced an open model, two datasets and four papers. Two cities now use the model in their spring planning. The approach could extend to small cities without engineering staff.<sup>a</sup></p><div class='evbox'><div><b>a.</b> Smith et al. (2023), Water Research <span class='note'>(Peer-reviewed)</span><br>Shows the model beats the city's old method.</div></div></div><div class='contrib'><p><span class='ctitle'>2. Training the next group of modellers.</span> <em>I co-led the training program.</em> Municipalities lack staff who can run these models. I ran a yearly summer school for municipal engineers. Sixty engineers have completed it. Eight cities now have a trained modeller on staff.<sup>a</sup></p><div class='evbox'><div><b>a.</b> Program report, 2024 <span class='note'>(Policy / practice uptake)</span></div></div></div><h2>Supervisory and mentorship activities</h2><p><b>Totals (last five years):</b> 4 doctoral students, 9 master's students, 2 postdoctoral fellows, 11 undergraduate researchers</p><p>PhD → Industry, National water non-profit, Evaluation. Recognition: Best-paper prize, 2024. Her role is built on methods developed in my group.</p><p>Every project begins with a written agreement on authorship and data, revisited at the midpoint.</p></div></body></html>
